you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme as energy bills for  many are set to rise again we look at  the cost of fossil fuels  versus renewable power the debate over  culling beavers in scotland  campaigners say they improve  biodiversity and flood management  for some farmers they're destroying  precious land  and the sailing competition where teams  race for more than just a win  on the water  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  now the cost of energy for millions of  families will be going up this autumn  after the regulator ofgem announced the  energy price cap will increase  it means bills rising by as much as 139  pounds a year  why well it's mainly to do with the  wholesale cost of fuel specifically  fossil fuels well the cost of gas is  increasing  surging fossil fuel prices are driving  up inflation for consumers  leading to that rise in the price cap  and that matters because we're still  using a significant amount of gas in our  electricity  supply and you can see that's the blue  line here  staying fairly consistent over the last  20 years or so  but great britain's electricity system  is undergoing a period of significant  change as we transition from fossil  fuels to renewables now there's been a  marked increase in wind and solar that's  shown by the green line here  while the use of coal that's the white  line has dropped off  almost entirely so that's the long-term  trend now if we look in more detail at  the first quarter of this year and last  year  we can see that most of our renewable  energy came from  bioenergy offshore and offshore that's  those first three blocks  solar and hydro both account for around  two percent that's the yellow and white  now overall in the first quarter of 2021  renewables provided just over  41 percent of our electricity the third  highest quarterly share on record but  will the move to more renewable energy  actually be any better for consumers  well our correspondent dan whitehead  joins me now so what does this mean for  consumers dan  well initially for consumers this really  will hit their pockets we're down in  devon and we've been speaking to  families right across the county  uh today who from october are braced uh  to  feel the impact of the rise in the  energy cap we've been with the jackson  family  in peyton in devon and they've told us  they are going to have to change  their lifestyle given the amount that  their bill is now going to go up by  so top of the list is obviously got to  pay all the bills  first like gas electric council tax  water bills all that sort of thing  then after that it's all your luxuries  to you know give your family a bit of  fun a bit of entertainment at the end of  the day we like our holidays we like  restaurants  we like going to the cinema um but  obviously  in the back of the mine there's going to  be less money for that now  the advice of course at the minutes is  that consumers can simply switch  to a better deal but the long-term  future in terms of reducing our energy  bills is going to be renewable  and green energy a recent report from  the international renewable energy  agency found that around two-thirds of  the new wind and solar farms that were  built right around the world  just last year are capable of creating  cheaper energy  than the cheapest newest coal plants  that are being created  so we've moved away from knowing that  simply renewable energy is better for  the environment also now it can create  cheaper energy so that will be the  future for people to reduce their bills  but for the time being  across the uk people will simply have to  shop around and switch  they can of course uh select to get an  energy provider that is green that comes  from renewable energy  whether or not that actually is  reflected in how much they pay each  month probably not yet that's going to  be a bit more time  so how do we make that transition from  fossil fuels to renewables  well let's bring in nicholas shaw uk  president of the national grid  welcome to you first of all what are the  cost implications for  individuals of this transition from  fossil fuels to renewables  the good news is we're already well into  the transition we're  way ahead of many countries and in terms  of cost  it's a complicated mixture of the good  news again  is that renewable energy is really free  once we get it once the infrastructure  is there we can then  use the power of the sun and the power  of the wind to generate the electricity  so it's free at the point of uh having  made the infrastructure investment  we paid for the infrastructure over a  long period so  overall we think that it is a really  good investment for um  consumers in the uk and how are we doing  with our infrastructure how much  renewable capacity do we have at the  moment to meet a rise in demand  we're doing really well as i said we're  amongst the leading company countries  in the world particularly in wind um  even we're hitting records in our  delivery  of electricity from offshore wind at the  moment in april we hit  a particular record and we know that  about four and a half million homes in  the uk are already powered by  renewable energy but there is more to do  and we will probably have to double  the amount of electricity we're going to  use by 2050 just because we'll have more  things that are connected to electricity  which means quite a lot more investment  and particularly in the next decade  really want to see a big step  change in the amount of offshore wind  generated electricity coming  onshore and it's interesting you talked  about the next decade because the prime  minister has made a pledge hasn't he  that  every home can be powered by wind power  within that time  frame is that really possible so  a big pledge isn't number one pledge in  his ten point plan the government's ten  point plan  was to deliver 40 gigawatts of offshore  wind by  2030 and at the moment we have just over  10 gigawatts so you can see a big step  up um  and that is feasible but we've got to  get going and there's a lot to do  so you first have to get planning  permission uh you have to then get the  financing in place  and then you have to do the construction  nicholas shaw thanks very much indeed  and don't forget you can see a more  detailed breakdown of the real-time  power mix in great britain anytime you  like on our website skynews.comclimit  wildfires are continuing to burn in  parts of the united states with a newly  ignited fire destroying at least  50 homes in northern california the  river fire which started on wednesday  has charred over 2 400 acres in nevada  and placer counties  authorities are warning the california  could be on track to suffer the worst  fire season  on record well that comes as new  pictures show the extent of devastation  in the gold rush town of greenville  the dixie fire which has burned for  three weeks now  destroyed 75 percent of the historic  californian  town among the damaged buildings were  saloons  the town museum and over a hundred homes  and in greece thousands of people have  been forced to leave their homes in  athens  after strong winds caused wildfires  there to spread rapidly  the fires have been worsened by a heat  dome a high pressure bubble trapping  heat within an area  it's seen parts of the country reach 47  degrees  and meteorologists warn climate change  will make these extreme weather events  more frequent in today's other climate  news cop 26 president alok sharma has  been accused of hypocrisy  after it was revealed he's flown to 30  countries in just  seven months the government says  face-to-face meetings are key  to successful negotiations at the  climate change conference being held in  glasgow later this year  but green party peer jenny jones  criticized the flights as excessive  saying the minister should have used  trains for some trips instead  france's highest court has fined the  government there 8.4 million  pounds for failing to improve air  quality  four years ago ministers were ordered to  reduce levels of nitrogen dioxide  in line with european standards but the  court ruled  insufficient measures have been taken  the court has threatened to issue a  similar fine every six months until  air pollution targets are met  and wine production in france could  slump by as much as 30 percent this year  its lowest level in decades  extreme weather made worse by climate  change meant vineyards were hit by  severe spring frosts and torrential  rainfall  champagne production will also be cut by  around half  after harvests were destroyed by mildew  fungus caused by the fluctuating weather  scotland's highest civil court is due to  issue a ruling soon on the right to kill  beavers rewilding charity trees for life  brought a case against nature scott for  allowing farmers to kill the wild  creatures  arguing control licences are being  issued too freely  the charity says beavers are vital to  improving biodiversity and boosting  natural flood management but for farmers  they can seriously damage  land as guys james matthews reports  beavers are being shot in parts of  scotland where they were released  without authorization  and where they disturb prime  agricultural land  i feel extremely frustrated louise  ramsey has them on her land  licenses to kill she says are issued too  readily  when they build their dams they bury  tons and tons of waterlogged wood  all of this is sequestering carbon the  the other thing is that climate change  as we know causes droughts and floods  beavers hold the water table steady  through both drug routes and floods  holding onto the water on the land in a  time of flood is really critical for  everybody and everything for all these  creatures and for humans  but for the dam builder there's a dam  buster  the people who shoot the beavers are  typically farmers and landowners  they're recognised as being a  biodiversity benefit and in some areas  that's absolutely the case there's no  doubt about that  however whether causing serious  agricultural damage  we need to be able to control them the  scottish government agency nature  scott insists it only issues licences to  kill  if it's satisfied it's the only option  campaigners disagree  and say that alternatives aren't fully  considered  they hope that if a judge agrees with  them fewer beavers will be shot  and more will be relocated from parts of  the country where they're not welcome  to parts where they are james matthews  sky news  perthshire now with all the excitement  of the olympics there's lots of focus on  how to make sport  more sustainable one event already  trying to do their bit is sail  gp teams in the international sailing  competition  don't only race in the water but on  sustainability  too the impact league runs alongside  the racing leaderboard with teams  rewarded for the actions they take  to reduce their overall carbon footprint  and we asked none other than great  britain's sailing legend sir ben ainslie  to explain more  [Music]  the impact league is it's it's really a  race for the teams  we race on the water for the trophy if  you like  but we're also racing for the impact  [Music]  you know we're all competitive animals  out there racing you know to try and win  on the water but  now we're we're racing to try and save  the planet  to try and save our oceans  we take it seriously we take it really  seriously you know the teams are already  they're out there monitoring what each  other are doing in terms of the  the sustainability approach  [Music]  you know we're going to need to see that  level of change  across all sports and across all society  if we're going to meet the targets that  we need to  to get on top of this global issue  we're pushing seriously hard from within  our own sport and within sales gp  and we expect the same from other sports  [Music]  well that's everything from us for today  but don't forget to check out the weekly  climate show  to find out more about the devastating  wildfires in turkey and what calls them  and you can find that on the sky  news snapchat channel and in this week's  episode of climatecast can video games  encourage us  to go green that's out wherever you  normally get your podcast from now  thanks for watching see you next week  [Music]

